(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-09-treatment-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 9: Treatment Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A randomized controlled trial compares two drugs in common use for the treatment of asthma. Three hundred patients were entered into the trial, and eligibility criteria were broad. No effort was made to blind patients to their treatment group after enrollment. Except for the study drugs, care was decided by each individual physician and patient. The outcome measure was a brief questionnaire assessing asthma-related quality of life. Which of the following best describes this trial?","choices":["Pragmatic trial","Large simple trial","Efficacy trial","Equivalence trial","Noninferiority trial"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This trial was built to mirror everyday practice: two drugs already in common use, broad eligibility, no blinding after enrollment, usual care for everything except the study drugs, and a patient-centered questionnaire outcome. That is a pragmatic trial, which asks whether a treatment works under ordinary circumstances. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.1"},{"q":"A randomized controlled trial compared angioplasty with fibrinolysis for the treatment of acute myocardial infarction. The authors state that “analysis was by intention to treat.” Which of the following is an advantage of this approach?","choices":["It describes the effects of treatments that patients have actually received.","It is unlikely to underestimate treatment effect.","It is not affected by patients dropping out of the study.","It describes the consequences of offering treatments regardless of whether they are actually taken.","It describes whether treatment can work under ideal circumstances."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients are analyzed according to the group they were randomized to, so the result shows the consequences of offering a treatment, whether or not patients take it. This reflects effectiveness in ordinary circumstances, where compliance is imperfect. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.2"},{"q":"In a randomized trial, patients with meningitis who were treated with corticosteroids had lower rates of death, hearing loss, and neurologic sequelae. Which of the following is a randomized comparison?","choices":["The subset of patients who, at the time of randomization, were severely affected by the disease","Patients who experienced other treatments versus those who did not","Patients who remained in the trial versus those who dropped out after randomization","Patients who responded to the drug versus those who did not","Patients who took the drug compared with those who did not"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Severity at the time of randomization is a characteristic that existed before treatment was assigned, so it was randomly allocated to both groups and comparing the severely affected patients in each group remains a randomized comparison. Randomization balances characteristics present at the start, not those that arise afterward. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.3"},{"q":"A patient asks for your advice about whether to begin an exercise program to reduce his risk of sudden death. You look for randomized controlled trials but find only observational studies about this question. Some are cohort studies comparing sudden death rates in exercisers with rates of sudden death in sedentary people; others are case control studies comparing exercise patterns in people who had experienced sudden death and matched controls. Which of the following is not an advantage of observational studies of treatments like these over randomized controlled trials?","choices":["Reported effects are for patients who have actually experienced the intervention.","It may be possible to carry out these studies by using existing data that was collected for other purposes.","The results can be generalized to more ordinary, real-world settings.","Treatment groups would have had a similar prognosis except for treatment itself.","A large sample size is easier to achieve."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, this is not an advantage of observational studies. Without randomization, people who choose or receive a treatment tend to differ in prognosis from those who do not, so confounding is difficult to avoid. Creating groups with similar prognosis except for treatment is the advantage of randomization. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.4"},{"q":"In a randomized controlled trial of a program to reduce lower extremity problems in patients with diabetes mellitus, patients were excluded if they were younger than age 40, were diagnosed before becoming 30 years old, took specific medication for hyperglycemia, had other serious illness or disability, or were not compliant with prescribed treatment during a run-in period. Which of the following is an advantage of this approach?","choices":["It makes it possible to do an intention-to-treat analysis.","It avoids selection bias.","It improves the generalizability of the study.","It makes an effectiveness trial possible.","It improves the internal validity of the study."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Strict inclusion and exclusion criteria (and a compliance run-in) make trial patients more alike and more likely to follow the program, so a real difference between treatments is easier to detect. That improves internal validity, the degree results are correct for the patients studied, at the cost of generalizability. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 25; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.5"},{"q":"Which of the following is not accomplished by an intention-to-treat analysis?","choices":["A comparison of the effects of offering the experimental treatments","A comparison more relevant to effectiveness questions","A comparison of the effects of actually taking the experimental treatments","A randomized comparison of treatment effects"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, this is not accomplished. Intention-to-treat analysis compares groups by assignment, so it does not isolate the effect of actually taking treatment. Studying patients by the treatment they really took treats the data like a cohort study, which is vulnerable to confounding. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.6"},{"q":"You are reading a report of a randomized controlled trial and wonder whether stratified randomization, which the trial used, was likely to improve internal validity. For which of the following is stratified randomization particularly helpful?","choices":["The study includes many patients.","One of the baseline variables is strongly related to prognosis.","Assignment to treatment group is not blinded.","Many patients are expected to drop out.","An intention-to-treat analysis is planned."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When a baseline characteristic is strongly related to prognosis, stratified randomization first divides patients by that characteristic and then randomizes within each stratum, guaranteeing both treatment groups have similar mixes of it. It is most useful for this and for smaller trials, where simple randomization might leave groups with different prognosis. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.7"},{"q":"A randomized controlled trial is analyzed according to the treatment each patient actually received. Which of the following best describes this approach to analysis?","choices":["Superiority","Intention-to-treat","Noninferiority","Open label","Per-protocol"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. In a per-protocol analysis, outcomes are attributed to the treatment patients actually received rather than the group they were randomized to. Because who takes treatment is no longer random, it loses randomization's protection against confounding. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.8"},{"q":"In a randomized controlled trial, a beta-blocking drug is found to be more effective than placebo for stage fright. Patients taking the beta-blocker tended to have a lower pulse rate and to feel more lethargic, which are known effects of this drug. For which of the following is blinding possible?","choices":["The patients' physicians","The investigators who assigned patients to treatment groups","The patients in the trial","The investigators who assess outcome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The investigators who assign patients to groups make that decision before any drug is started, so no side effect can reveal the assignment to them. This is allocation concealment, in which the people assigning patients are kept unaware of which treatment comes next. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 24; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.9"},{"q":"Which of the following best describes “equipoise” as the rationale for a randomized trial of two drugs?","choices":["The drugs are known to be equally effective.","One of the drugs is known to be more toxic.","Neither drug is known to be more effective than the other.","Although one drug is more effective, the other drug is easier to take with fewer side effects."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Equipoise means the scientific community has no conclusive evidence that either drug is better; there may be opinions and partial evidence, but no consensus. Randomization is ethical only in this state of uncertainty, and the trial is done to resolve it. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.10"},{"q":"Antibiotic A is the established treatment for community-acquired pneumonia, but it is expensive and has many side effects. A new drug, antibiotic B, has just been developed for community-acquired pneumonia and is less expensive and has fewer side effects, but its efficacy, relative to drug A, is not well established. Which of the following would be the best kind of trial for evaluating drug B?","choices":["Superiority","Crossover","Cluster","Noninferiority","Equivalence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A noninferiority trial determines whether a new treatment is unlikely to be less effective than an established one. It fits antibiotic B, which has advantages (cheaper, fewer side effects) but whose efficacy relative to antibiotic A is not established. It usually needs a larger sample size than a superiority trial because differences may be small. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.11"},{"q":"In a randomized controlled trial of two drugs for coronary artery disease, the primary outcome is a composite of acute myocardial infarction, severe angina pectoris, and cardiac death. Which of the following is the main advantage of this approach?","choices":["There are more outcomes events than there would be for any of the individual outcomes.","All outcomes are equally affected by the interventions.","The trial has more generalizability.","Each of the individual outcomes is important in its own right.","If one outcome is infrequent, others make up for it."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Combining related, clinically important outcomes produces more outcome events than any single outcome would, which increases the trial's ability to detect a real effect if one exists. The drawback is that the intervention may affect components differently, and the composite can mask this. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.12"},{"q":"In a randomized controlled trial comparing two approaches to managing children with bronchiolitis, baseline characteristics of the 200 children in the trial are somewhat different in the two randomly allocated groups. Which of the following might explain this finding?","choices":["“Bad luck” in randomization","A breakdown in allocation concealment","Both","Neither"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bad luck in randomization and a breakdown in allocation concealment can each leave baseline characteristics different between the groups. Small differences are expected by chance; the challenge is deciding how large a difference must be to raise concern. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.13"},{"q":"Which of the following is usually learned from a Phase III drug trial?","choices":["The relationship between dose and efficacy","Rates of uncommon side effects","Efficacy or effectiveness","The dose range that is well tolerated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Phase III trials are larger, randomized trials intended to give definitive evidence of efficacy or effectiveness and identify some common side effects. These are the trials usually published in the clinical literature. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.14"},{"q":"Which of the following is the main advantage of randomized controlled trials over observational studies of treatment effects?","choices":["Fewer ethical challenges","Prevention of confounding","Resemble usual care","Quicker answer","Less expensive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Randomization creates groups that would have the same outcome rates, on average, were it not for the treatment, so confounding is prevented. In observational studies, systematic differences in groups other than the treatment factor make confounding difficult to avoid; this is why randomized trials are the gold standard for treatment efficacy. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 9.15"}]};
